# XDeNovo {{ project_name }}

## Repository Role

- This repository owns the `{{ project_name }}` Compute MCP Service.
- It is a private downstream called through the XDeNovo Gateway, not a separate public MCP
  endpoint.
- It was created from a one-time scaffold and independently owns all subsequent dependency, CI,
  Docker, documentation, and service-code maintenance.
- Use the canonical
  [Platform architecture](https://github.com/XDenovo/platform/blob/main/docs/architecture.md) and
  [approved technology stack](https://github.com/XDenovo/platform/blob/main/docs/techstack.md) as
  supplemental platform-wide context.

## Architecture and Security Guardrails

- Accept only the short-lived internal credential issued by Gateway. Never accept, store, log, or
  forward an external access token.
- Own this service's database Schema, Job and Artifact metadata, object-storage namespace,
  Temporal Namespace, and Task Queue. Do not access another service's resources.
- Keep resource ownership checks in this service even after runtime authentication establishes a
  caller identity.
- Submit long-running work through durable Workflows and execute it in Workers or isolated
  Compute Job containers, not inside an MCP request.
- Keep service-specific Tools, scientific dependencies, domain logic, migrations, Workflows, and
  Activities here. Move infrastructure genuinely shared by all services into `mcp-runtime`.
- Keep credentials, JWTs, database passwords, object-storage secrets, and presigned URLs out of
  logs and exception text.

## Toolchain and Setup

- Python 3.13 and uv 0.11.30.
- `mcp-runtime` and FastMCP provide the authenticated MCP service layer; pytest runs tests, Ruff
  handles linting and formatting, ty handles type checking, and prek manages Git hooks.
- `pyproject.toml` owns tested dependency compatibility ranges; `uv.lock` owns exact resolved
  dependency state.

```bash
uv sync --locked
```

Inside an initialized Git checkout, install the configured hooks:

```bash
uv run prek install
```

Use uv for dependency changes and commit both `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`:

```bash
uv add <package>
uv add --dev <package>
uv remove <package>
uv lock
```

`mcp-runtime` is a direct Git dependency pinned to a released tag. The lockfile must resolve that
tag to an immutable commit. To upgrade, change only to another released tag, run
`uv lock --upgrade-package mcp-runtime`, execute the full validation matrix including Docker, and
commit `pyproject.toml` with `uv.lock`. Never use `main`, a branch, a local path, or an unreleased
commit as the final dependency.

## Service Assembly and Configuration

- `{{ package_name }}.service.create_service(settings, *, jwks_transport=None)` is the
  side-effect-free production assembly seam. Keep `jwks_transport` limited to tests; do not add
  arbitrary Runtime or FastMCP keyword forwarding.
- Importing the package, `service`, or `__main__` must not read environment, validate deployment
  settings, open clients, or start the Server.
- `python -m {{ package_name }}` constructs `RuntimeSettings` with the generated service ID
  `{{ project_name }}`, assembles the service, and calls `run_server()`. Do not make
  `MCP_RUNTIME_SERVICE_ID` able to override this identity.
- Startup requires `MCP_RUNTIME_AUTH__ISSUER` and `MCP_RUNTIME_AUTH__JWKS_URL`. Runtime does not
  automatically load `.env` files.
- Loopback defaults to `127.0.0.1:8000`. A non-loopback deployment must explicitly provide both
  `MCP_RUNTIME_SERVER__HOST` and a non-wildcard JSON
  `MCP_RUNTIME_SERVER__ALLOWED_HOSTS`; deployment hostnames do not belong in this repository.

## Development Conventions

- Keep MCP handlers thin and separate transport, authorization, domain logic, persistence,
  Workflow submission, and Artifact handling.
- Derive database, storage, and Temporal boundaries from service configuration rather than
  accepting arbitrary identifiers for another service.
- Keep sync and async operations explicit and avoid blocking the FastMCP event loop.
- Give database engines, external clients, streams, Workers, and task containers explicit
  lifecycle and cleanup paths.
- Use `mcp-runtime` configuration, authentication, Server, lifecycle, and observability
  foundations rather than copying them here.

## Testing and Validation

Run the same checks as generated CI:

```bash
uv sync --locked
uv run --no-sync ruff check .
uv run --no-sync ruff format --check .
uv run --no-sync ty check
uv run --no-sync pytest
uv build
```

Focused static checks:

```bash
uv run --no-sync ruff check <changed-path>
uv run --no-sync ruff format --check <changed-path>
```

Focused tests:

```bash
uv run --no-sync pytest tests/unit
uv run --no-sync pytest tests/integration
uv run --no-sync pytest tests/integration/test_authentication.py
```

- Put configuration-free imports and process assembly behavior in `tests/unit`.
- Put authenticated Bearer/Streamable HTTP behavior in `tests/integration`.
- Keep fixtures function-scoped and each async client inside an explicit `async with` cleanup
  boundary.
- Use only the public `mcp_runtime.testing` credential factory, client, and rejection assertion.
  Do not copy JWT/RSA/JWKS helpers, inspect private keys, parse SDK `ExceptionGroup` internals, or
  use an authentication-bypassing in-memory Server transport.
- Tests do not need a live Gateway or open a network port. Do not establish an arbitrary global
  coverage threshold for service-specific scientific code.

## Build and Deployment

Build the package and container with:

```bash
uv build
docker build -t {{ project_name }} .
```

Local startup:

```bash
MCP_RUNTIME_AUTH__ISSUER=https://api.xdenovoai.com/ \
MCP_RUNTIME_AUTH__JWKS_URL=http://gateway.internal/.well-known/jwks.json \
uv run python -m {{ package_name }}
```

The container command already runs this module. Container deployment must inject authentication
and non-loopback Server settings at runtime; the image must remain neutral to production
hostnames, networking, and Secrets.

Production composition, private networking, secrets delivery, and operational runbooks belong to
`XDenovo/platform-deploy`.

Readiness/liveness contracts and Docker runtime smoke testing are not established by this
scaffold.

## Git and Pull Requests

- Treat the Issue as the implementation specification and the PR as the result report.
- Follow Conventional Commits.
- Use the XDenovo organization-default Issue and PR templates.
- Preserve unrelated working-tree changes, and stage only the explicit paths intended for a
  commit.
- Changes to Tool contracts, Job or Artifact schemas, Workflow inputs, or configuration are
  compatibility changes and require corresponding tests and migration notes.
